  "guideline_verdict": {
    "correct_variant_designation": "Complete-case evaluation with boundary-clamped or unclamped inputs",
    "rationale": "Per the 2013 ACC/AHA Guideline (Goff et al.): 1. Inputs must be complete: clinical risk scoring tools in practice are applied when a clinician has actual laboratory measurements, not imputed population medians. 2. Clamping fallback to 5.0% (Pass 4) was an artificial implementation artifact that misclassified 303 high-risk individuals (with true CVD mortality of 4.62% vs 1.72% baseline) as low risk (5.0%), falsely deflating PCE discrimination from 0.7800 to 0.7391. 3. When evaluated correctly (either unclamped or boundary-clamped, on complete cases or full cohort), PCE achieves C-index of 0.773-0.780 and 5-year AUROC of 0.767-0.774. 4. Crucially, the conclusion of whether ML achieves a 'statistically significant gain' over PCE on identical inputs depends entirely on this choice: under the correct unclamped or boundary-clamped variants, the paired difference 95% CI includes zero, demonstrating NO CLEAR DIFFERENCE between ML Cox and published PCE.",
    "significant_gain_depends_on_variant": true
  }
